      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114807/assets/aeb0c87a33cf30494636f0a8f8d411033ba064385ad04065abff184752ea99d7.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the subset $A$ of $U={-4,-3,-2,-1,0}$ whose indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ is shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)={\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $-4$ and $0$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A={-4,0}.$",
